115: ## 2. Logic Chain
116: 
117: 1. **Word Boundary Matching for Repeated Entities**:
118:    - `item.original` detected by native ML NER can be a word (e.g. `"Dan"`, `"Rob"`), a multi-word phrase (`"John Doe"`), an international name (`"José"`, `"张伟"`), or start with a symbol (`"+1-555"`).
119:    - In JavaScript regex, `\b` matches an ASCII word boundary. For ASCII words (`/^\w+$/`), `\b${escaped}\b` matches discrete word occurrences without matching inside compound words (`\bDan\b` matches `"Dan"` but does not match `"Danger"`).
120:    - For strings starting/ending with non-ASCII letters (e.g. `"José"` where `é` is non-ASCII) or non-word symbols, Unicode-aware lookarounds `(?<![\p{L}\p{N}_])` and `(?![\p{L}\p{N}_])` provide safe boundary checking.
121:    - We formulate `createEntityWordBoundaryRegex(original)`:
122:      ```ts
123:      function createEntityWordBoundaryRegex(original: string): RegExp {
124:        const escaped = escapeRegex(original);
125:        const leading = /^\w/.test(original) ? "\\b" : (/^[\p{L}\p{N}_]/u.test(original) ? "(?<![\\p{L}\\p{N}_])" : "(?<!\\S)");
126:        const trailing = /\w$/.test(original) ? "\\b" : (/[\p{L}\p{N}_]$/u.test(original) ? "(?![\\p{L}\\p{N}_])" : "(?!\\S)");
127:        const flags = (/[\p{L}\p{N}]/u.test(original) && !/^[\x00-\x7F]+$/.test(original)) ? "gu" : "g";
128:        return new RegExp(`${leading}${escaped}${trailing}`, flags);
129:      }
130:      ```
131:    - In `src/services/redactor.ts`, we replace the dangerous `while` loop with a single-pass global `.replace()`:
132:      ```ts
133:      currentText = currentText.replace(regex, () => {
134:        labelCounters[item.label] = (labelCounters[item.label] || 0) + 1;
135:        const placeholder = `[${item.label}_${labelCounters[item.label]}]`;
136:        allRedactions.push({ ... });
137:        return placeholder;
138:      });
139:      ```
140:    - Single-pass `.replace()` with a callback operates left-to-right on the original string, never rescanning its own replacement outputs, which guarantees termination with zero risk of infinite loops.
141: 
142: 2. **Seeding `labelCounters` from Native Results**:
143:    - Before executing repeated entity replacement, `labelCounters` is pre-seeded with the highest index from `nativeResult.items`:
144:      ```ts
145:      for (const item of nativeResult.items) {
146:        const matchNum = item.placeholder.match(/_(\d+)\]$/);
147:        if (matchNum) {
148:          labelCounters[item.label] = Math.max(
149:            labelCounters[item.label] || 0,
150:            parseInt(matchNum[1], 10)
151:          );
152:        }
153:      }
154:      ```
155:    - Any newly discovered repeated occurrence receives `labelCounters[item.label] + 1`, guaranteeing that placeholder numbers are strictly monotonically increasing and never collide with existing entities.
156: 
157: 3. **Sequential Numbered Placeholders for Secret Overlay Rules**:
158:    - In `src/rules/default-rules.ts`, remove `mask: () => "[REDACTED:API_KEY]"` from `api-key-generic`.
159:    - In `src/services/redactor.ts` Phase 1:
160:      ```ts
161:      let masked: string;
162:      if (rule.mask) {
163:        masked = rule.mask(match);
164:      } else {
165:        const labelPrefix =
166:          rule.nativeLabel ??
167:          (rule.category === "api-key"
168:            ? "API_KEY"
169:            : rule.category.toUpperCase().replace(/[-\s]/g, "_"));
170:        labelCounters[labelPrefix] = (labelCounters[labelPrefix] || 0) + 1;
171:        masked = `[${labelPrefix}_${labelCounters[labelPrefix]}]`;
172:      }
173:      ```
174:    - Matches generate `[API_KEY_1]`, `[API_KEY_2]`, etc.
175:    - In `currentRestoreMap`:
176:      Each unique placeholder maps to its specific original value: `[API_KEY_1] -> key1`, `[API_KEY_2] -> key2`.
177:    - **Backwards Compatibility**: When exactly 1 secret is redacted (`labelCounters["API_KEY"] === 1`), we also map `[REDACTED:API_KEY] -> singleKey` in `currentRestoreMap`. This ensures that existing tests (e.g. `tests/e2e/tier5-adversarial.test.ts:431`) and any LLM prompts formatted with `[REDACTED:API_KEY]` restore seamlessly, while multi-secret prompts require discrete placeholders to restore.
178: 
179: 4. **Test Assertion Bug Resolution**:
180:    - In `tests/stress-challenger.test.ts:69`, changing `expect(result.text).not.toContain("ger")` to `expect(result.text).not.toContain("]ger")` resolves the contradiction while strictly validating that `"Danger"` was preserved as a regular word and not mutilated into `[GIVEN_NAME_2]ger`.
181:    - In `tests/stress-challenger.test.ts:229`, updating `expect(result.text).toContain("[REDACTED:API_KEY]")` to `expect(result.text).toContain("[API_KEY_1]")` aligns the test with the required numbered placeholder design.
182:    - In `tests/redactor.test.ts` lines 19 & 66, updating `[REDACTED:API_KEY]` to `[API_KEY_1]` aligns unit tests with the new numbered format.
